<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Always Pack Lunch. – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Trip to the Beach</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Always Pack Lunch.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Facilitate a simple discussion among classmates.<br>concept: Link historical events to current events for class discussions.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate a discussion about beach trips by asking simple questions, providing sentence starters, and using gestures (e.g., saying, 'What do you bring to the beach?' while pointing to sunscreen or showing a packing checklist).
            Can link historical beach-related events to current activities with guided comparisons (e.g., saying, 'Look at the pictures of people at the beach. Which one is from the past and which one is from today?' while students identify clothing and activities from each period).
            Can encourage classmates to compare beach activities from the past and present using structured prompts and gestures (e.g., 'How is it different now?' while pointing to an old-fashioned bathing suit).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows two large pictures: one of people at a beach in the past (e.g., black and white, old-fashioned swimsuits, simple activities) and another of people at a beach today (e.g., colorful, modern gear, beach ball, cooler). The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and "What do people always pack for the beach?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models comparing the pictures, using gestures and simple sentences. "Look! In the past, people wore this [points to old swimsuit]. Today, people wear this [points to modern swimsuit]. We always pack sunscreen now. They did not always pack sunscreen." The teacher uses imperative form to initiate discussion: "Please, look at the pictures." The teacher emphasizes /b/ in 'beach' and /p/ in 'pack'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this picture from now or long ago?" (Teacher points to past picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do people do the same things at the beach today and in the past?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Trip to the Beach".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue comparing a grandparent's beach trip with a child's planned trip. The dialogue integrates 'present simple', 'going to', 'imperatives', and target vocabulary (beach, towel, sunscreen, bucket, cooler, swim, relax, pack). For example:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">**Grandma:** "When I was little, we went to the beach. We always collected shells and played in the sand. We didn't have a big cooler. We sometimes packed a small picnic."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">**Child:** "Wow, Grandma! I am going to the beach this weekend! I am going to swim and build a sandcastle. I always pack lunch, a towel, and sunscreen. Please, bring your sun hat!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Facilitating communication: Linking historical events to current events for class discussions by identifying similarities and differences in beach activities and items.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Who goes to the beach now?" "Who went to the beach long ago?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match picture cards of items (cooler, old bucket) to "Grandma" or "Child."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False questions: "Grandma always packed a big cooler." "The child is going to swim."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using Present simple, 'going to', Imperatives, and the target vocabulary (beach, towel, sunscreen, sand, shell, swim, relax, build, pack) to compare and facilitate discussions about past and present beach activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Gap-fill Comparison):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks. Students fill in the blanks using target vocabulary and correct verb forms (present simple) to compare two pictures (past vs. present beach scenes). E.g., "In the past, people _____ (wear) long clothes. Now, we _____ (wear) swimsuits." "They _____ (collect) shells. We _____ (build) sandcastles."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled (Sentence Starters for Comparison):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives them picture cards (e.g., an old beach umbrella vs. a modern one, a simple bucket vs. a fancy one). Students use sentence starters to compare: "Then, they had _____. Now, we have _____." "People used to _____. Today, people _____." The teacher encourages them to use gestures and ask, "How is it different?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Facilitating Discussion):</b> In pairs, students receive a "Beach Packing List - Past" and "Beach Packing List - Present." One student describes their list, the other asks questions. Then they switch roles. They use prompts to facilitate discussion and comparison: "What do you bring to the beach?" "What did people bring then?" "Please, tell me about your list." "How is it different now?" (Encourage polite imperatives and question words: What, Where, When).</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Trip to the Beach".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Project Explanation and Discussion Facilitation (21st Century Skills Project 2: Trip Storyboard).</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group has their "Trip Storyboard" about 'A Trip to the Beach' using 'going to'. Groups exchange storyboards. Each group takes turns explaining *their* storyboard to the *other* group, acting as facilitators.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group A explains their storyboard to Group B. They use "going to" (e.g., "We are going to the beach early.", "I am going to swim.") and present simple (e.g., "I always pack lunch.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group A members facilitate understanding by asking questions to Group B (e.g., "What do you see here?", "Do you understand our plan?"), providing sentence starters ("We are going to..."), and using gestures (pointing to pictures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group A then encourages Group B to compare elements of their storyboard to beach trips from the past or to their own experiences (e.g., "Is this like beach trips today?", "How is it different from your trip?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then switch roles, with Group B explaining and facilitating for Group A.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, listening, providing support, and encouraging the use of target vocabulary and grammar, especially imperatives ("Please, ask a question," "Tell them about your cooler.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the storyboard explanation/mediation activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students, in their groups, successfully explain their "Trip Storyboard" to another group and facilitate a brief discussion about it, including comparing past/present beach activities or packing items.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist - Yes/No/Sometimes):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use "going to" to describe future plans (e.g., "I am going to swim.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use present simple for routines (e.g., "I always pack lunch.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use target vocabulary (beach, towel, sunscreen, pack, swim, cooler, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can facilitate discussion by asking simple questions (e.g., "What do you bring?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can provide simple sentence starters to classmates.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use gestures to aid understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of /b/ and /p/ sounds is generally intelligible.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a strip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I helped my friends understand my storyboard by __________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I learned that beach trips from the past are different because __________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy about explaining your storyboard and helping your friends understand?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about comparing past and present beach activities with your classmates?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in facilitating the storyboard explanations and discussions? Were students able to use the target grammar and vocabulary effectively in a mediation context?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks, especially for linking historical and current concepts at this grade level? Were the gestures and sentence starters sufficient?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which students demonstrated strong mediation skills (e.g., good questioning, clear explanations, effective use of gestures)? Which students need more practice?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This concludes the "A Trip to the Beach" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, possibly reviewing travel vocabulary or focusing on other leisure activities, building on students' ability to describe plans and routines.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>